@@ -90,3 +90,43 @@ See `proto-tools/notes/error-handling.md` for the full tool-side contract.
 | `proto_language/core/generator.py` | Generator assignment and proposal validation |
 | `proto_language/utils/serialization.py` | `format_pydantic_error()` |
 | `proto_language/utils/scoring.py` | `MAX_ENERGY`, `MIN_ENERGY` constants (re-exported from `proto_language.utils`) |
+
+## Scoped execution observation
+
+`proto_language.core.sampling_observation_scope(observer, control)` observes the
+complete public `Generator.sample` orchestration without replacing methods.
+Custom generators must implement `_sample`, preserving the public base method.
+`PortfolioProgram` composes independent native Program lanes serially and retains
+ordinary per-lane failures; cancellation always propagates.
+
+A shared `proto_tools.utils.execution_observer.ExecutionControl` accepts a
+monotonic deadline or explicit `cancel()`. Checks are cooperative, not preemption
+of a blocking kernel. A host still needs its process deadline. Scoped observers
+are context-local and must be propagated explicitly to other threads/processes.
+
+Managed hosts may opt into `require_scoped_calls=True` on both observation
+scopes. This rejects requests/sampling from unpropagated child threads only while
+the exclusive managed scope is active. Native ToolPool partitions already copy
+ContextVars; native paired MMseqs threads now do likewise. Log-drain, heartbeat,
+progress and standalone process threads do not execute registered public calls.
+Copied scope state is shared and expires on owner exit. Ordinary unobserved use
+and independently scoped non-managed contexts keep their original behavior.
+
+### Observation publication failure
+
+Failure callbacks cannot hide a native exception or cancellation: the exact
+original object and existing cause escape, with the callback failure retained
+as an exception note. In managed scopes, a throwing failure callback also
+latches publication uncertainty and cancels the shared ExecutionControl.
+
+A throwing completed callback in a managed scope raises
+`ObservationPublicationError`, a BaseException, with the publication failure as
+cause. PortfolioProgram checks both scoped cancellation controls before its
+first lane and each subsequent lane, so neither completed nor failed publication
+uncertainty can authorize a sibling lane to begin. Hosts must provide the same
+explicit ExecutionControl to both managed scopes. Successfully recorded ordinary
+scientific lane failures retain the normal per-lane portfolio policy.
+
+Callback failure objects are also retained on the original exception as
+`proto_observation_callback_failure`, supporting Python 3.10 (without notes) and
+malformed note storage without changing the original exception identity/cause.
